# Flutter and Dart Code Generation Rules

## Core Architecture Requirements

- Always follow the clean architecture pattern with domain, data, and presentation layers
- Strictly adhere to TypeScript configurations defined in `analysis_options.yaml`
- Never use `dynamic` type explicitly or implicitly - all types must be properly defined
- Use snake_case for all filenames (e.g., `user_profile.dart`, not `UserProfile.dart`)
- Prefer feature-based organization over layer-based organization
- Use proper dependency injection with Riverpod

## Code Verification Process

Before finalizing any generated code:

1. Check current package versions in pubspec.yaml to ensure compatibility
2. Verify API usage against actual implementations in the project
3. Search for current best practices using the firecrawl MCP server

## Directory Structure and Organization

Follow this feature-based clean architecture:

- `lib/features/[feature_name]/`: Feature-specific code
  - `domain/`: Entities, repository interfaces, failures, validation
  - `data/`: Repository implementations, data sources, DTOs
  - `presentation/`: Screens, widgets, view models
- `lib/core/`: Shared code used across features
  - `error/`: Error handling (failures, exceptions)
  - `util/`: Utility functions and extensions
  - `network/`: Network-related code (API clients, interceptors)
- `lib/app/`: Application-level code
  - `router/`: Routing configuration
  - `theme/`: Theme configuration
  - `localization/`: Localization configuration

## State Management with Riverpod

When implementing Riverpod providers:

1. Use AsyncNotifier for async operations
2. Use StateNotifier for synchronous state management
3. Organize providers by feature
4. Use proper error handling with AsyncValue
5. Implement proper loading states

## Authentication & Protected Routes

When implementing authentication:

- Create an AuthRepository in the domain layer
- Implement proper error handling with Either<Failure, Success>
- Use secure storage for tokens
- Implement proper token refresh mechanisms
- Use go_router for protected routes

## Testing

- Write unit tests for all repository implementations
- Write widget tests for UI components
- Write integration tests for key user flows
- Use mocktail for mocking dependencies
- Follow the testing templates in docs/TESTING_TEMPLATES.md

## Bug Fixing & Code Generation Workflow

- For every code generation or bug fix, set a checkpoint so changes can be reversed if needed
- When executing several steps to fix a bug, always generate a memory so the solution can be easily applied in other contexts
- Use all available memory-oriented tools and MCP servers to maintain multiple copies of solutions
- When fixing bugs or eliminating errors, focus ONLY on the bug being fixed in the file in question unless otherwise instructed or necessary
- Do not make enhancements during bug fixing

## Type Management

- Always check the current code base FIRST before creating new types to avoid duplicates
- Follow the prescribed directory structure and methodology to prevent type duplication
- Use freezed for immutable models
- Use json_serializable for JSON serialization

## Package Management

- Use Flutter's pub for package management
- Run `flutter pub get` after adding new dependencies
- Run `dart run build_runner build --delete-conflicting-outputs` for code generation
